%U A401895 3,3,4,3,4,4,4,4,3,3,3,3,4,3,4,4,4,3,3,2,3,3
%N A401895 Number of distinct decimal digits in the product of n and its digit reversal.
%C A401895 The sequence counts the distinct decimal digits occurring in A061205(n). The first occurrences of the values 1 through 10 are at indices 1, 4, 13, 17, 119, 147, 1069, 1089, 10396, and 14979, respectively.
%C A401895 Every value from 2 through 10 occurs infinitely often.
%C A401895 For the values 3 through 10, the product at the first occurrence contains the digit 0, so by the formula below a(10^m*n) = a(n) for all m >= 1; for the value 2, a(10^m) = 2.
%C A401895 For 1 <= n < 10^6, a(n) = 1 only for n = 1, 2, 3. It is conjectured that these are the only such indices.
%F A401895 a(n) = A043537(A061205(n)).
%F A401895 Let r(n) denote the decimal digit reversal of n.
%F A401895 For any n >= 1 and m >= 1, a(10^m*n) = a(n) if 0 occurs in n*r(n), and a(10^m*n) = a(n) + 1 otherwise.
%F A401895 If n is not divisible by 10, then a(n) = a(r(n)).
%e A401895 a(10) = 2 since 10*1 = 10 has two distinct digits, 0 and 1. Note that a(10) != a(r(10)) = a(1) = 1, so the symmetry a(n) = a(r(n)) requires that n is not divisible by 10.
%e A401895 a(12) = 2 since 12*21 = 252 has two distinct digits, 2 and 5.
%e A401895 a(17) = 4 since 17*71 = 1207 has four distinct digits.
%e A401895 a(119) = 5 since 119*911 = 108409 has five distinct digits.
%e A401895 a(14979) = 10 since 14979*97941 = 1467058239 contains all ten decimal digits.
%o A401895 (Python)
%o A401895 def a(n): return len(set(str(n*int(str(n)[::-1]))))
%o A401895 (PARI)
%o A401895 a(n) = #Set(digits(n*fromdigits(Vecrev(digits(n)))));
%Y A401895 Cf. A004086, A043537, A061205.
%K A401895 nonn,base,easy,new
